        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "k"
        }
      ],
      "why": "unrolled_k has 0 designs on the merged front and best luts_plus_ffs=362, well above iterative's 257. However, it has 29 feasible designs out of 38 evals, so it is not dead. The key question is whether a smaller k (2-4) with tighter data_width/n_iter can close the area gap. I restrict k to 2-4 (k=5-8 adds area without helping the area objective) and focus data_width/n_iter on 12-18 to match the accuracy sweet spot. This is a secondary exploration to confirm whether unrolled_k can contribute any non-dominated points or to definitively rule it out, freeing the front for iterative-only coverage."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The merged front is entirely owned by iterative (13/13 designs, best area 257). unrolled_k, pipelined, and pipelined_m have zero front membership, confirming iterative is the dominant family for this area-minimising spec. My plan therefore concentrates 55% of the budget on iterative, narrowing the search to the data_width/n_iter 12-18 region where accuracy_bits can be pushed toward the HV reference of 10 while keeping luts_plus_ffs near the current best of 257. The remaining 45% goes to unrolled_k with k restricted to 2-4, to either find a few non-dominated points that extend the front (e.g., slightly higher area but notably higher accuracy_bits) or to confirm it cannot compete. Both families share the same accuracy model, so the data_width/n_iter/guard ranges are identical; the only structural difference is k, which affects area and throughput but not accuracy. The throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is non-binding for both families at these parameter values since even iterative at N=18 gives ~5.5 cycles/result, and at a conservative 100 MHz that is ~18 MSPS. The binding constraints are max_abs_err <= 2^-10 and the area objective, both of which are addressed by the focused ranges."
}

